WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 200: Ancient Egypt: The Land of the Pharaohs, Imhotep, and Joseph's Granaries

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.360
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.560
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.680
[Rebecca] Today we cross the threshold into a monumental new saga, The Great Biblical Empires.

4
00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:21.440
[Rebecca] For the next 12 episodes, we explore the ancient superpowers whose laws, conquests, and monuments shaped world history and intersect with the timeless pages of the biblical record.

5
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.440
[Rebecca] We begin with the mother of ancient civilizations, ancient Egypt.

6
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:33.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the gift of the Nile, the miracle of Imhotep, and the granaries of Joseph.

7
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:41.120
[George] Five thousand years ago, Greek historian Herodotus famously observed that Egypt is an absolute gift of the Nile.

8
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:53.280
[George] Surrounded on all sides by the merciless, waterless expanses of the Libyan and Arabian deserts, Egyptian civilization existed solely because of the miraculous rhythmic pulse of the world's longest river.

9
00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:04.080
[George] Every summer, monsoon rains in the Ethiopian highlands swelled the Blue Nile, sending torrents of mineral-rich volcanic silt cascading north into the Mediterranean Delta.

10
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:12.800
[George] In August, the river overflowed its banks, submerging the valley under a warm, fertile sheet of water during the sacred season of Akhit.

11
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:21.040
[George] Egyptian priests and astronomers monitored stone nilometers carved along the riverbank at Elephantine, measuring the rise in cubits.

12
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.040
[George] A 16-cubit rise meant boundless abundance, while 12 cubits warned of lean scarcity.

13
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:40.240
[George] When the floodwaters receded in October, they left behind a thick layer of nutrient-dense black silt that the Egyptians called Kemet, the Black Land, in contrast to the hostile red desert sands known as Deserit.

14
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:55.920
[George] Upon this miraculous soil, Egyptian farmers cultivated fields of emmer wheat, barley, flax, and tall thickets of papyrus reeds, yielding two agricultural harvests a year and providing fibers for paper scrolls, sandals, and river boats.

15
00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:04.200
[George] The predictable abundance of the river gave the Egyptians an unshakable sense of cosmic order, harmony, and eternal resurrection known as Mad.

16
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:23.960
[George] Protected by impenetrable deserts to the east and west, the cataracts to the south, and the Mediterranean Sea to the north, Egypt developed for centuries in splendid geographical isolation, giving birth to a civilization of breathtaking architectural genius, religious devotion, and administrative mastery.

17
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:34.040
[George] In the third millennium before Christ, during Egypt's old kingdom, human architecture underwent a quantum leap under the genius of a single man, Imhotep.

18
00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:50.600
[George] Serving as the chief royal architect, high priest of the sun god Ra at Heliopolis, and grand vizier to Pharaoh Joser of the Third Dynasty, Imhotep was a polymath of such extraordinary intellect that later generations deified him as the god of medicine and wisdom.

19
00:02:50.600 --> 00:03:03.240
[George] Prior to Imhotep, Egyptian pharaohs and nobles were buried in rectangular, flat-roofed mudbrick tombs known as mastabas, which were vulnerable to erosion, flash floods, and grave robbers.

20
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:14.840
[George] Imhotep conceived a revolutionary architectural vision, building an indestructible stone stairway to the heavens so the pharaoh's soul could ascend among the imperishable circumpolar stars.

21
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:32.520
[George] At Sakara, overlooking the ancient capital of Memphis, Imhotep stacked six successively smaller stone mastas atop one another, quarrying and carving limestone blocks with bronze chisels to create the world's first step pyramid, soaring over two hundred feet into the desert sky.

22
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:48.080
[George] Beneath this artificial stone mountain lay an astonishing subterranean labyrinth, nearly four miles of carved corridors, burial vaults, and gallery chambers adorned with vibrant blue faience tiles depicting Joser running the Heb sed festival of rejuvenation.

23
00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:58.960
[George] Imhotep surrounded the pyramid with a monumental complex of stone temples, ceremonial courtyards, and fluted stone columns resembling bundles of papyrus reeds.

24
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:15.520
[George] Beyond architecture, Imhotep authored ancient medical treatises describing clinical anatomical diagnoses, sutures, and surgical treatments thousands of years ahead of ancient Greece, proving that mortal men could construct eternal monuments to bridge the earth and the heavens.

25
00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:24.560
[George] A century after Imhotep, during the Fourth Dynasty, Egyptian engineering achieved its absolute pinnacle on the limestone plateau of Giza.

26
00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:37.680
[George] Pharaoh Kufu commissioned the Great Pyramid, an astronomical and mathematical marvel consisting of 2,300,000 individual limestone and granite blocks, each weighing between two and fifteen tons.

27
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:51.360
[George] Rising 481 feet above the desert, the Great Pyramid remained the tallest artificial structure on planet Earth for over 3,800 years until the spire of Lincoln Cathedral was completed in medieval England.

28
00:04:51.360 --> 00:05:06.480
[George] The monument was originally encased in 20 acres of polished Tura limestone casing stones, cut with such microscopic precision that a knife blade could not fit between the joints, gleaming like a dazzling white mirror under the blazing Saharan sun.

29
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:16.640
[George] Deep within its core sat the Grand Gallery and the King's Chamber, roofed with 50-ton red granite slabs, transported hundreds of miles north from the quarries of Aswan.

30
00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:22.720
[George] Popular Hollywood myth long portrayed the pyramids as the brutal work of whips and enslaved captives.

31
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:44.600
[George] In historical and archaeological fact, modern excavations at the workers' village of Height El Gurab have uncovered the permanent bakeries, breweries, and medical facilities of a bustling city of twenty thousand skilled, free Egyptian craftsmen, stonemasons, and seasonal farm laborers who worked during the annual Nile flood when fields were submerged.

32
00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:54.280
[George] These free workers viewed the construction as a sacred civic and religious duty, building a monumental tomb aligned to true cardinal north within a fraction of a degree.

33
00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:04.120
[George] Guarded by the enigmatic Great Sphinx and flanked by the pyramids of Kafir and Menkora, Giza stood as the eternal symbol of Egyptian engineering perfection.

34
00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.840
[George] While the Nile was the source of life, it held a terrifying dark side.

35
00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:17.880
[George] If the monsoon rains failed in Africa, the river did not flood, drying the soil into cracked dust and bringing catastrophic starvation.

36
00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:30.280
[George] On the island of Elephantine near Aswan, archaeologists discovered the famous Famine Stila, a massive granite inscription carving an ancient memory of seven consecutive years of terrifying famine.

37
00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:33.720
[George] The Nile had not come in my time for seven years.

38
00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:39.400
[George] Grain was scarce, seeds were dry, everything which one had to eat was in deficit.

39
00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:44.280
[George] The infant wept, the young man faltered, the hearts of the old were crushed.

40
00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.760
[George] Every man stole from his neighbor.

41
00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:54.280
[George] This harrowing historical reality directly matches the dramatic biblical account of the patriarch Joseph in the book of Genesis.

42
00:06:54.280 --> 00:07:09.640
[George] Betrayed by his envious older brothers and sold into slavery for twenty pieces of silver to Midianite camel traders carrying spices and balm down to the Delta, seventeen-year old Joseph was purchased by Potiphar, captain of Pharaoh's royal guard.

43
00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:19.200
[George] Managing Potiphar's entire estate with flawless integrity, Joseph was falsely framed by Potiphar's scorned wife and cast into the royal dungeon.

44
00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:27.920
[George] In prison, Joseph's divine gift for dream interpretation correctly foretold the fortunes of Pharaoh's imprisoned chief cupbearer and baker.

45
00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:33.600
[George] Two years later, Pharaoh was troubled by twin nightmares that his magicians could not decipher.

46
00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:34.960
[George] Seven fat.

47
00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:48.160
[George] Sleek cows grazing in the reed grass were suddenly devoured by seven gaunt, sickly cows emerging from the Nile, followed by seven plump heads of grains swallowed by seven withered heads blasted by the east wind.

48
00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:58.080
[George] Remembering the Hebrew prisoner, the cupbearer spoke, and Joseph was shaved, clothed in clean robes, and brought from the dungeon into the presence of the throne.

49
00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.720
[George] Joseph revealed the divine warning.

50
00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:08.160
[George] Seven years of unprecedented agricultural abundance would be followed immediately by seven years of devastating global famine.

51
00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:27.200
[George] Impressed by Joseph's supernatural wisdom and practical administrative clarity, Pharaoh removed his own golden signet ring and placed it upon Joseph's finger, dressed him in royal vestures of fine white linen, placed a heavy gold chain of honor around his neck, and proclaimed him grand vizier of Egypt.

52
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:35.360
[George] Thou shalt be over my house, and according unto thy word shall all my people be ruled, only in the throne will I be greater than thou.

53
00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.280
[George] Pharaoh bestowed upon him the Egyptian name Zaphanath Panah.

54
00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:46.720
[George] Meaning God speaks and he lives, and gave him in marriage Asenet, daughter of Potiphara, high priest of Aon.

55
00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:52.400
[George] Joseph initiated the world's first massive, nationwide food reserve logistics system.

56
00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.880
[George] For seven years of bumper harvests.

57
00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:11.000
[George] Joseph supervised the construction of colossal brick granaries and domed silos in every provincial capital across upper and lower Egypt, collecting a twenty percent tax on all agricultural production until the stored grain was beyond measure, like the sand of the sea.

58
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:19.960
[George] When the terrifying drought struck North Africa and the Levant, turning fields to ash and drying mountain wells, the granaries of Egypt opened.

59
00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:26.920
[George] Caravans of desperate people from Canaan, Syria and Arabia travelled to the Nile to purchase life-saving wheat.

60
00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:38.520
[George] Among the foreign supplicants were Joseph's own ten older brothers, who bowed low with their faces to the earth before the Egyptian ruler they did not recognize, fulfilling Joseph's childhood dreams.

61
00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:50.680
[George] Testing their repentance through a planted silver goblet, Joseph finally broke down in tears of forgiveness, sending his Egyptian attendants away and weeping so loudly that Pharaoh's palace heard it.

62
00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.200
[George] I am Joseph your brother, whom you sold into Egypt.

63
00:09:54.200 --> 00:10:01.400
[George] Now therefore be not grieved, nor angry with yourselves, for God did send me before you to preserve life.

64
00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:11.800
[George] Jacob and his entire family of seventy souls migrated to Egypt, settling with their flocks in the lush, fertile pasturelands known as the Land of Goshan.

65
00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:21.560
[George] Centuries later, Egypt's Middle Kingdom fractured into division, paving the way for the most humiliating trauma in Egyptian history, the Hyksas invasion.

66
00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:34.600
[George] Around 1650 BC, a federation of West Semitic warriors from Canaan and the Levant, known to Egyptians as the Hekokasut, or rulers of foreign lands, swept across the Sinai Peninsula.

67
00:10:34.600 --> 00:11:00.320
[George] Armed with revolutionary military technologies that the Egyptians had never seen, composite recurve bows made of horn and wood with double the range of Egyptian self-bows, horse-drawn two-wheeled war chariots, and bronze scimitar battle axes, the Hyksos seized Lower Egypt and established their royal capital at Avaris in the eastern delta, ruling the native Egyptians for over a century as the 15th dynasty.

68
00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:09.920
[George] Many biblical scholars believe it was during this Semitic Hyksos era that Joseph and the Hebrews enjoyed high favor and administrative authority in the Delta court.

69
00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:20.640
[George] But native Egyptian princes in Thebes nursed their pride, adopting the war chariot, manufacturing composite bows, and launching a brutal war of national liberation.

70
00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:34.000
[George] Led by Pharaoh 2nd Enertau, whose mummified skull in the Cairo Museum bears horrific, shattered battle axe wounds from combat, and his fierce sons Kamos and Amos I, the Theban warriors mobilized.

71
00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.280
[George] Queen Ahotip rallied the troops, awarded the golden flies of valor for bravery.

72
00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:52.480
[George] Amos I stormed the fortress of Avaris, chased the Hyksos across the Sinai, and besieged their stronghold of Sharuhan in southern Canaan for three years, founding the glorious 18th dynasty and the new kingdom.

73
00:11:52.480 --> 00:12:01.760
[George] Yet this war of liberation birthed a fierce, xenophobic military state that viewed all foreign Semitic peoples living in their borders with deep suspicion.

74
00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:10.960
[George] With the dawn of the new kingdom, Egypt abandoned its traditional defensive isolationism to become a ruthless, expansionist imperial superpower.

75
00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:25.040
[George] Operating from their southern religious capital at Thebes, modern Luxor, the warrior pharaohs marched victorious armies south into Goldrich Nubia past the Fourth Cataract and northeast across the Levant to the banks of the Euphrates River.

76
00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:26.880
[George] Queen Hatshepsut.

77
00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:48.440
[George] Ruling as female pharaoh with full royal regalia and a ceremonial false beard, dispatched a famous fleet of trading galleys down the Red Sea to the mysterious land of Punt, returning with 31 living myrrh trees, frankincense, ebony, gold, and leopard skins to build her breathtaking terraced mortuary temple at Der el Bahari.

78
00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:59.000
[George] Her stepson and nephew, Thutmos Ii, celebrated by modern historians as the Napoleon of Egypt, led seventeen victorious military campaigns into the Levant.

79
00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:14.840
[George] At the Battle of Megiddo in 1457 BC, Thutmos executed a daring tactical gamble, marching his chariots through a narrow, perilous mountain pass while his generals urged caution, surprising and routing a massive Canaanite coalition.

80
00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:21.640
[George] He established Egyptian garrisons from Gaza to Lebanon, turning Egypt into the wealthiest empire on earth.

81
00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:32.280
[George] Royal treasuries overflowed with tributary gold, poured into the gargantuan hypostyle halls, towering granite obelisks, and sphinx-lined avenues of Karnak Temple.

82
00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:47.560
[George] Yet, as the empire flourished on foreign conquests and imperial pride, the resident Hebrew population in the Delta pastures multiplied into a vast, distinct multitude, arousing the paranoid fear of a new dynasty of warrior pharaohs.

83
00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:56.600
[Rebecca] Ancient Egypt stood at the pinnacle of imperial majesty, its monuments defying time, and its armies ruling the sands of the Middle East.

84
00:13:56.600 --> 00:14:02.120
[Rebecca] Yet within the mud brick villages of the Nile Delta, a gathering storm was brewing.

85
00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:10.680
[Rebecca] Next week, we continue our ancient Egypt saga with episode 201: Moses, the Ten Plagues, and the Iron Chariots of Pharaoh.

86
00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:19.280
[Rebecca] The bitter enslavement, the burning bush in Midian, the supernatural clash of deities before Pharaoh's throne, and the miraculous parting of the Sea of Reeds.

87
00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:24.320
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

